// Direction. A method declares which side of its receiver its call site
// sits on: plain methods read left-to-right (receiver on their left);
// `right-to-left` methods take their receiver from the right. Associativity
// is the same axis for now: left-associative ≡ left-to-right,
// right-associative ≡ right-to-left.
//
// An expression that contains a right-to-left method — or the bare marker
// `</` — is read from its right end: the rightmost token is the anchor, and
// every method folding onto a receiver must read the direction being walked.
// Methods of both directions can share one expression only POINTING APART —
// `rtl X ltr` reads as both (rtl X) and (X ltr); running towards each other
// they have no single reading, and mixing them needs parentheses.

external left-to-right
external right-to-left
external left-associative
external right-associative
external right-to-left </

external test-middle
external left-to-right test-left
external right-to-left test-right
external right-associative test-assoc
external left-to-right right-to-left test-bidir

// reading left-to-right (the default): the receiver is to the left
// info: test-left fired on `test-middle`
test-middle test-left

// a right-to-left method flips the whole expression — the anchor is the
// rightmost token and the receiver is to its right
// info: test-right fired on `test-middle`
test-right test-middle

// right-associative is the same flag
// info: test-assoc fired on `test-middle`
test-assoc test-middle

// the bare marker flips an expression with no directional method in it,
// transparently returning what the flipped walk read
test-middle </

// marker and method together: the marker is read first (rightmost), the
// rest is walked right-to-left through it
// info: test-right fired on `test-middle`
test-right test-middle </

// a method flagged for both directions folds either way — it does not flip
// anything by itself, it just reads along
// info: test-bidir fired on `test-middle`
test-middle test-bidir

// info: test-bidir fired on `test-middle`
test-bidir test-middle </

// methods pointing apart share the operand between them: the expression is
// read right-to-left up to it, and left-to-right from it
// info: test-right fired on `test-middle`
// info: test-left fired on `test-middle`
test-right test-middle test-left

// chains too: `A x B x C y D` with x right- and y left-associative reads
// right-to-left through the x's up to the shared operand C, and
// left-to-right from C through y
// info: test-right fired on `test-middle`  (the inner x, around A)
// info: test-right fired on `test-middle`  (the outer x, on C)
// info: test-left fired on `test-middle`   (y, on C)
test-middle test-right test-middle test-right test-middle test-left test-middle

// running towards each other, they have no single reading
// error: Cannot mix `test-left`, `test-right` in a single infix expression with mixed associativity, use parenthesis to mix them.
test-middle test-left test-right test-middle

// the same holds anywhere in a chain
// error: Cannot mix `test-left`, `test-right` in a single infix expression with mixed associativity, use parenthesis to mix them.
test-middle test-left test-middle test-right test-middle

// the marker after a left-to-right method runs towards it just the same
// error: Cannot mix `test-left`, `</` in a single infix expression with mixed associativity, use parenthesis to mix them.
test-middle test-left </

// read right-to-left, a plain (left-to-right) method cannot fold onto a
// receiver
// error: Found a method `test-middle` on `test-middle` but it wasn't flagged as right-to-left.
test-middle test-middle </
